WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:09.910
So I want to share with
you a story of evolution.

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:13.360
And as you can see, this
evolution, unsurprisingly,

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:15.930
starts at sea.

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:17.170
See, this is me.

00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:19.170
And I just started
out my master's degree

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:22.170
in Marine Ecology and I
went out to the Red Sea

00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:23.790
to do some fish sampling.

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:26.220
And when I was there,
I was completely

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:28.800
overwhelmed by the
beauty of the coral reef

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:30.900
with all these
beautiful shapes, colors

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:33.090
and really nice looking fish.

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:35.760
But when I was done
sampling and looking

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:37.950
at all these crazy fish
and it was time for me

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:39.960
to go in and fill in the data.

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:44.370
I was welcomed by this ugly,
unintuitive, hard to navigate

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:46.320
Microsoft Access forms.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:48.725
And this is the starting
point for our journey.

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:54.010
This journey is a
walkthrough of how

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:59.080
I moved from using Microsoft
Access to using Google Sheets

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:05.349
and using R to do our own
lab custom-made database.

00:01:05.349 --> 00:01:07.570
And I hope that
whether you're using

00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:09.790
Microsoft Access
now or Google Sheets

00:01:09.790 --> 00:01:12.550
or any of these
different alternatives

00:01:12.550 --> 00:01:16.408
for making data forms,
you will find some lessons

00:01:16.408 --> 00:01:17.950
that I've learned,
something that you

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:21.060
could use in your workflow.

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:22.980
So the first step
in this walkthrough

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:25.340
was to understand our needs.

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:26.870
And to understand
our needs, I just

00:01:26.870 --> 00:01:30.240
have to say a few words
about how exactly we operate.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:33.320
So we are, as I said,
a marine ecology lab

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:36.320
and we are going
using scuba diving

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:38.490
to do some underwater surveys.

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:43.700
So we go out there, as you can
see here, with a water resistant

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:45.630
piece of paper and a pencil.

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:47.690
And we just do some
records of the fish

00:01:47.690 --> 00:01:51.250
species and their abundance
when we are underwater.

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:53.630
So why do we need--

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:55.750
Why did we need Microsoft
Access to do that?

00:01:55.750 --> 00:01:58.760
So first we know we
needed a big data set.

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:01.000
So as you can see
from these photo,

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:03.180
these are taken
several years apart.

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:05.260
We are doing this
for a long time

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:08.050
and we have no intention
of stopping anytime soon.

00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:10.330
And you can see why.

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:14.480
Another thing, we need a
strict--for our database--

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:16.960
we need a strict form
of data validation.

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:19.710
Because this fella.

00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:21.870
Their name is Pomacanthus imperator.

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:24.742
And if you don't write that
exactly as the bottom row,

00:02:24.742 --> 00:02:26.950
you're going to have a lot
of problems down the road.

00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:29.760
And that's true for
any of the cells

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:32.580
that we have in our data form.

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:36.120
Most importantly, we
need relational data.

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:39.330
So our data is structured
in a relational format

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:41.380
where we have the trip,

00:02:41.380 --> 00:02:44.490
the topmost level is
mainly the location

00:02:44.490 --> 00:02:47.910
where we are diving in
the world, and the date.

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:51.090
Then we have the site
which is more close

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:55.110
up of who is diving where
exactly in the actual dive

00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:56.590
site.

00:02:56.590 --> 00:03:00.790
Then we have the transect, the
underwater environmental data.

00:03:00.790 --> 00:03:04.390
So that's the visibility,
the depth, the substrate.

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:08.380
Lastly, we have the observation
itself, the species of the fish

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:11.930
and its abundance.

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:14.360
When we used Microsoft
Access, unknowingly,

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:16.160
we made some sacrifices.

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:19.310
So first we had really
bad user interface.

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:23.690
So for us, it was really hard
to work with Microsoft Access.

00:03:23.690 --> 00:03:26.390
I remember clicking the
wrong button a few times,

00:03:26.390 --> 00:03:28.450
getting this really
loud error message.

00:03:28.450 --> 00:03:32.390
It was a hell to, at least
for me, it was a hell to work.

00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:35.480
Another thing that was really
detrimental to our workflow

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:38.300
is that we couldn't figure out
how to do simultaneous data

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:39.910
entry for our needs.

00:03:39.910 --> 00:03:46.670
So what happened is we had
to actually email the database

00:03:46.670 --> 00:03:49.460
file from one person
to another, and so we

00:03:49.460 --> 00:03:51.200
took turns filling in the data.

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:53.420
So sometimes it could
take more than a

00:03:53.420 --> 00:03:56.000
week until it was
your turn to do so,

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:00.100
and it was really detrimental
to the quality of the data.

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:05.290
Lastly, since we didn't
build the database ourselves,

00:04:05.290 --> 00:04:09.040
it was really hard for us to
make any customization needed

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:10.750
for our needs.

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:14.320
So for example, we are working
on both the Mediterranean Sea

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:15.329
and the Red Sea.

00:04:15.329 --> 00:04:17.829
They are completely different
seas with completely different

00:04:17.829 --> 00:04:18.850
animals.

00:04:18.850 --> 00:04:20.269
We need different protocols.

00:04:20.269 --> 00:04:23.050
And for this we need
a different database.

00:04:23.050 --> 00:04:25.960
So that was also a problem.

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:28.180
Taking into account
all of these things

00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:32.350
we set out to create
our own database.

00:04:32.350 --> 00:04:34.390
So what we did was we create.

00:04:34.390 --> 00:04:35.900
So that was our prototype now.

00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:40.960
Okay, we have this giant
Google spreadsheet

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:42.740
for an entire expedition.

00:04:42.740 --> 00:04:46.660
And every day we had someone
in charge of manually creating

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:49.673
different spread
different worksheets

00:04:49.673 --> 00:04:51.340
within the spreadsheet,
different works,

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:54.190
different worksheets for each
of the different surveyors

00:04:54.190 --> 00:04:56.560
of that day.

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:00.280
So when I created this
Google Sheet prototype,

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:02.540
I needed to take some
drastic measures.

00:05:02.540 --> 00:05:05.940
So I won't have any user error
and didn't know how to do that.

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:11.530
So my first idea was to have
this really horrendous color

00:05:11.530 --> 00:05:12.430
coding.

00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:16.450
And I'm going to show you we
had this bright red trip data.

00:05:16.450 --> 00:05:17.870
So these are different forms.

00:05:17.870 --> 00:05:19.580
All the forms are
in different colors.

00:05:19.580 --> 00:05:22.300
So we have this
trip data in red.

00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:25.720
Then we add this
yellow, is it yellow

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:31.270
here, this yellow site data,
the green transect data,

00:05:31.270 --> 00:05:34.430
and lastly, that
blue observer data.

00:05:34.430 --> 00:05:36.380
So as you can see,
really nice colors.

00:05:36.380 --> 00:05:40.810
And to connect this--to
connect all of these,

00:05:40.810 --> 00:05:44.860
the observers themselves
had to copy and paste IDs

00:05:44.860 --> 00:05:47.920
so we could create
this relational data.

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:51.310
But then, so what happens
is for every expedition

00:05:51.310 --> 00:05:54.070
we have this: a lot of
different worksheets

00:05:54.070 --> 00:05:58.750
within a spreadsheet that, as you
can see, some red, some blue.

00:05:58.750 --> 00:06:00.130
It's a whole mess.

00:06:00.130 --> 00:06:04.000
But this is where we
used googlesheets.

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:05.480
It was googlesheets
at the time,

00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:07.540
Now it's googlesheets4.
We could

00:06:07.540 --> 00:06:10.240
read by that color coding.

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:13.900
So, and then using that
ID that the users had

00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:16.720
to copy and paste,
we could use dplyr

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:21.100
to connect it all to a flat
data set that we can work with.

00:06:21.100 --> 00:06:23.000
Now, I know it's a mess.

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:26.265
And if you have this, if you have
this expression right now, then

00:06:26.265 --> 00:06:28.390
yeah, this is the expression
that all my colleagues

00:06:28.390 --> 00:06:30.910
had when I presented that
idea and I told them,

00:06:30.910 --> 00:06:32.380
let's stick with it.

00:06:32.380 --> 00:06:37.450
It can't be worse than
what we currently have.

00:06:37.450 --> 00:06:38.510
It wasn't worse.

00:06:38.510 --> 00:06:41.060
But the thing is, it
doesn't have to be perfect.

00:06:41.060 --> 00:06:41.690
All right?

00:06:41.690 --> 00:06:45.010
So, this is us.

00:06:45.010 --> 00:06:47.240
This is crit 2019.

00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:49.180
And I know it doesn't
look like that,

00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:52.470
but we are currently hard
at work filling in the data.

00:06:55.300 --> 00:06:58.330
So as you can see, we're
doing that together.

00:06:58.330 --> 00:06:59.750
So that's an important thing.

00:06:59.750 --> 00:07:02.710
We managed to solve
one big problem, which was

00:07:02.710 --> 00:07:04.510
the simultaneous data entry.

00:07:04.510 --> 00:07:07.370
So now that we can work
online, as you can see,

00:07:07.370 --> 00:07:08.120
it's the blue one.

00:07:08.120 --> 00:07:09.590
So,

00:07:09.590 --> 00:07:12.220
once we manage to
solve this problem

00:07:12.220 --> 00:07:14.020
and I know that the
prototype works,

00:07:14.020 --> 00:07:17.830
it's time for us to adjust, to
make the necessary adjustments,

00:07:17.830 --> 00:07:23.350
to make this data form database
much better to work with.

00:07:23.350 --> 00:07:26.020
So how did we do that?

00:07:26.020 --> 00:07:28.630
Mainly by increasing our
dependency of googledrive

00:07:28.630 --> 00:07:30.700
and googlesheets4 package.

00:07:30.700 --> 00:07:34.240
So we have a great tool
for actually working

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:36.310
with Google Drive
and Google Sheets,

00:07:36.310 --> 00:07:39.790
and we managed to automate
the entire process.

00:07:39.790 --> 00:07:44.170
So what we did at first,
we had an offline file

00:07:44.170 --> 00:07:48.580
that replaced that old
yellow and red worksheets

00:07:48.580 --> 00:07:51.190
that we had. Now they
are offline.

00:07:51.190 --> 00:07:56.750
And from this, we can set out
to automate the whole thing.

00:07:56.750 --> 00:08:01.550
And we use googledrive for
the housekeeping and navigation,

00:08:01.550 --> 00:08:05.090
and for the manipulation
we used googlesheets4.

00:08:05.090 --> 00:08:09.020
More specifically,
we had drive_mkdir()

00:08:09.020 --> 00:08:12.820
to create individual folders
for every sampling day.

00:08:12.820 --> 00:08:16.640
So now instead of having one
big Google Spreadsheets that's

00:08:16.640 --> 00:08:20.720
just somewhere on Drive,
we have this hierarchy

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:24.650
when we have an
expedition directory

00:08:24.650 --> 00:08:28.380
and within it for each day
we have a different folder.

00:08:28.380 --> 00:08:34.320
So it's much more organized
and gs4_create(), this function,

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:38.570
we used to upload that
offline file from which

00:08:38.570 --> 00:08:40.809
we create the metadata.

00:08:40.809 --> 00:08:43.900
We use drive_find()
and drive_cp() in order

00:08:43.900 --> 00:08:46.420
to copy and paste
pre-existing templates

00:08:46.420 --> 00:08:50.630
that we had to today's folder.

00:08:50.630 --> 00:08:55.210
So then we use sheet_copy()
and range_write() in order

00:08:55.210 --> 00:08:59.870
to manipulate those
pre-existing templates.

00:08:59.870 --> 00:09:05.440
(bless you) to manipulate
these pre-existing templates

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:07.790
into whatever's
necessary for today.

00:09:07.790 --> 00:09:11.320
So change in site
numbers, hour, et cetera.

00:09:11.320 --> 00:09:13.720
Lastly, what
I really liked

00:09:13.720 --> 00:09:15.820
is that we have
drive_share() and we

00:09:15.820 --> 00:09:18.460
use this function
in order to send out

00:09:18.460 --> 00:09:20.710
specific email
for specific users

00:09:20.710 --> 00:09:23.440
so they can only
change their own data,

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:25.900
so they can only fill
out their own form.

00:09:25.900 --> 00:09:28.480
And it's really good because
they don't have to mess around

00:09:28.480 --> 00:09:30.230
with anything in the database.

00:09:30.230 --> 00:09:34.480
And they can't accidentally
erase or change

00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:35.810
someone else's data.

00:09:35.810 --> 00:09:38.830
And for them it's really easy
because all they have to do now

00:09:38.830 --> 00:09:41.560
is get an email and they
sit down

00:09:41.560 --> 00:09:43.800
and they fill out the forms.

00:09:43.800 --> 00:09:46.260
So this is what we get.

00:09:46.260 --> 00:09:49.650
This is a folder for a
day and we have the metadata

00:09:49.650 --> 00:09:50.440
up there.

00:09:50.440 --> 00:09:53.100
And as you can see, we have
individual spreadsheets

00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:56.140
for each of the
different observers.

00:09:56.140 --> 00:10:00.820
And in order to do that,
I had to be

00:10:00.820 --> 00:10:03.580
be creative in a way.

00:10:03.580 --> 00:10:06.380
And let's zoom in into
one of the spreadsheets.

00:10:06.380 --> 00:10:10.360
So first thing as you can see,
the colors are much better.

00:10:10.360 --> 00:10:12.190
They're not really
bright anymore.

00:10:12.190 --> 00:10:16.660
And I wanted to have a
great, a strong connection

00:10:16.660 --> 00:10:21.700
between the environmental
data and the fish data.

00:10:21.700 --> 00:10:23.450
So we're doing ecology,
which is basically

00:10:23.450 --> 00:10:26.800
the relationship between a
species and its environment.

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:29.770
So we wanted to have this
demonstrated in our database,

00:10:29.770 --> 00:10:31.610
in our forms.

00:10:31.610 --> 00:10:32.730
To do that,

00:10:32.730 --> 00:10:36.800
you can see columns A and B
don't follow the same logic

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:41.330
as columns C through H, which
some people would say

00:10:41.330 --> 00:10:43.670
is unorthodox and incorrect.

00:10:43.670 --> 00:10:47.780
And for those of you who
can't see that A and B,

00:10:47.780 --> 00:10:51.000
each row does not
represent an observation.

00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:52.820
It is a long format.

00:10:52.820 --> 00:10:56.420
But once you realize that you
can go from this long format

00:10:56.420 --> 00:11:01.600
into a wide format, fairly
easily using the tidyverse,

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:05.050
then it opens up a whole world
of possibilities for you.

00:11:05.050 --> 00:11:07.960
For example,

00:11:07.960 --> 00:11:12.010
on another project where
we do quadratic sampling.

00:11:12.010 --> 00:11:15.040
I managed to take the
exact piece of paper

00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:22.090
that surveyors go to [in] the field,
and I managed to copy it almost

00:11:22.090 --> 00:11:24.910
verbatim into a digital form.

00:11:24.910 --> 00:11:27.580
Making the transition
from the actual survey

00:11:27.580 --> 00:11:32.120
data from the actual real
world data to the digital data

00:11:32.120 --> 00:11:35.510
much more intuitive
for my users.

00:11:35.510 --> 00:11:40.510
I encourage you
all to think about how

00:11:40.510 --> 00:11:44.620
user-oriented your data
forms are at the moment

00:11:44.620 --> 00:11:47.440
because I know, I've been taught
as well, that you should make

00:11:47.440 --> 00:11:50.890
your data as computer readable
as possible for analysis,

00:11:50.890 --> 00:11:55.930
visualizations, et cetera, but
at the same time, all of us

00:11:55.930 --> 00:11:59.680
were taught how easy it is
to manipulate the data shape.

00:11:59.680 --> 00:12:03.250
So we could all stand
to benefit from having

00:12:03.250 --> 00:12:05.690
more user-oriented data.

00:12:05.690 --> 00:12:09.520
So we could then get, so we
could minimize the errors

00:12:09.520 --> 00:12:13.000
from those users because it will
be much more intuitive for them

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:17.140
to fill in the data forms and
it will make our data much

00:12:17.140 --> 00:12:20.370
better at the end.

00:12:20.370 --> 00:12:22.420
So, this was our project.

00:12:22.420 --> 00:12:24.560
This is how it went
for quite a while.

00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:26.590
This worked quite well.

00:12:26.590 --> 00:12:30.480
It was really automated
and we worked for two years

00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:32.740
or so on this type of forms.

00:12:32.740 --> 00:12:36.300
But as we continue
to use this, we

00:12:36.300 --> 00:12:39.090
realized that we need to
make additional changes

00:12:39.090 --> 00:12:43.140
because as we kept
using this database,

00:12:43.140 --> 00:12:46.650
so did our needs and
our needs change.

00:12:46.650 --> 00:12:50.760
So we realized that
having an offline file

00:12:50.760 --> 00:12:53.250
was creating a bottleneck,
because we needed

00:12:53.250 --> 00:12:55.020
to have someone
who knows exactly

00:12:55.020 --> 00:12:57.930
how to type in the
specific observer's

00:12:57.930 --> 00:13:00.870
name and all kinds of stuff.

00:13:00.870 --> 00:13:04.950
Then we decided to put
that on the cloud as well.

00:13:04.950 --> 00:13:09.540
And then using the googlesheets
gs4_browse() function,

00:13:09.540 --> 00:13:13.800
we can open up the browser
directly from RStudio

00:13:13.800 --> 00:13:16.830
and then it opens up
something like that.

00:13:16.830 --> 00:13:20.530
and then you just fill
in with that validation,

00:13:20.530 --> 00:13:23.820
you fill in who are the
observers for today?

00:13:23.820 --> 00:13:27.430
So as you can see, I
took this opportunity

00:13:27.430 --> 00:13:30.340
to thank all my colleagues
here and my collaborators

00:13:30.340 --> 00:13:33.260
whose feedback was really
what drove this thing forward.

00:13:33.260 --> 00:13:35.980
So while I did the coding
and I presented the idea

00:13:35.980 --> 00:13:38.410
and everything,
their feedback is

00:13:38.410 --> 00:13:39.710
what drove this thing forward.

00:13:39.710 --> 00:13:41.590
If it wasn't for
them, we would still

00:13:41.590 --> 00:13:44.740
be stuck in that ugly
color phase, honestly.

00:13:44.740 --> 00:13:47.500
So thanks for that.

00:13:47.500 --> 00:13:51.250
Another thing, since we built
this thing from the ground up,

00:13:51.250 --> 00:13:54.310
now it's really easy for
us to make adjustments

00:13:54.310 --> 00:13:55.570
whenever we need.

00:13:55.570 --> 00:13:58.660
So, for example, let's say
we have another project now

